{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Answer the user question using only the selected evidence below.\nDo not assume facts that are absent from the selected evidence. If the evidence is\ninsufficient, return UNKNOWN rather than inventing a state or action.\n\nSelected evidence:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I’ve been reorganizing my movie and music collection lately and it got me thinking: what do you see as the biggest pros and cons of how people access entertainment now—streaming, digital downloads, rentals, libraries, etc.?\n[assistant] These days the biggest advantage is sheer convenience. You can pull up almost anything instantly, on any device, without worrying about physical storage or discs wearing out. It’s also great for discovery—you can stumble onto artists or films you’d never have found in the old model.\n\nThe trade‑off is that you don’t really *own* most of what you access. Streaming catalogs shift, digital purchases can disappear if rights change, and you’re dependent on subscriptions that keep creeping up in price. There’s also something a bit less satisfying about having no tangible collection, and it’s harder to guarantee long‑term access compared to a shelf of discs or a library checkout where the item is yours for the moment, no conditions attached.\n\nSo the modern approach wins on ease and variety, but you give up permanence and some of the charm of collecting.\n[user] I generally avoid streaming subscriptions since I prefer owning physical copies of my movies and music. Given that, what are the best ways to balance convenience with actually owning what I watch/listen to—are digital downloads worth it, or are things like Blu-rays/CDs and library rentals still the most reliable?\n[assistant] If true ownership matters to you, physical media is still the most reliable by a wide margin. A Blu‑ray or CD won’t vanish because a license expired, and you get the highest, most consistent quality. It’s also the only format where you control long‑term access without depending on a company’s servers.\n\nDigital downloads can be a decent middle ground, but only if they’re genuinely DRM‑free. Stores like Bandcamp or certain niche film distributors let you download files you can back up and keep, which gets you some of the convenience of digital without giving up ownership. Most mainstream movie stores, though, are really just selling a license tied to an account, so they’re less “ownable” than they look.\n\nLibraries are great for supplementing your collection when you don’t need to own something permanently. They’re reliable, cheap, and often have editions that aren’t on streaming.\n\nSo if ownership is your priority, physical media plus selective DRM‑free digital downloads gives you the best balance, with libraries filling in the gaps.\n[user] If I stick mostly to Blu-rays and CDs, what’s the best way to add some “digital convenience” without turning into a subscription person—ripping discs to a home media server, buying DRM-free files, something else? Also, any pitfalls to watch out for with ripping/backups and long-term storage?\n[assistant] A good balance is to keep buying your Blu‑rays and CDs, then rip them for your own private use so you can stream them around your home the way a subscription service would—just without giving up ownership. A small home server or something like Plex or Jellyfin works well for this, and you only have to set it up once. If you occasionally buy DRM‑free downloads from places that genuinely let you keep the files, that can fill in gaps without pushing you into subscription territory.\n\nThe main things to watch out for are pretty simple. Make sure you’re following your local laws about ripping for personal use. Keep at least two backups of your digital library—ideally on drives stored in separate places—so a single disk failure doesn’t wipe everything out. And every few years, check your drives and migrate the files to new ones; hard drives don’t last forever, and silent bit‑rot can sneak in if you never touch the data.\n\nIf you stay on top of backups and occasionally refresh your storage, a ripped library can be nearly as reliable as the discs themselves while giving you all the day‑to‑day convenience of streaming.\n[user] Could you recommend a practical setup for ripping and serving a disc-based library at home (hardware + software), with a backup plan that isn’t overkill? I’m mostly Blu-rays and CDs, and I’d like something I can use on a TV and phones on my home network.\n[assistant] A simple, solid setup is to use a small, low‑power computer as your media box—something like an Intel NUC, a used mini‑PC, or even a NAS that can run apps. Pair it with a decent external Blu‑ray drive for ripping. On the software side, MakeMKV and a good encoder like HandBrake will handle Blu‑rays, and any standard ripping software will work for CDs. For serving the files, Plex and Jellyfin are the easiest to live with and will stream cleanly to TVs, phones, and tablets on your home network.\n\nFor backups, you don’t need anything elaborate. Keep your master files on the media box or NAS, then mirror the whole library onto a single external hard drive that you plug in every so often to refresh the backup. If you want a bit more safety without going overboard, add one more backup drive you store somewhere else in your home. Replace the drives every few years so you don’t get caught by aging hardware.\n\nThat combination—one small machine, one good Blu‑ray drive, straightforward software, and two rotating backup drives—is usually enough to give you convenience, reliability, and long‑term control without turning it into a big technical project.\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I’m trying to get better at living with less stuff without feeling like I’m constantly depriving myself—do you have any practical system for deciding what to keep, what to donate, and how to organize what’s left so it doesn’t creep back into chaos?\n[assistant] You can think of it as a repeating loop rather than a one‑time purge: notice, choose, then reset.\n\nStart by pulling out a small category at a time—like just your jackets or just your mugs—and actually interact with each item. Ask yourself whether you use it regularly or whether it genuinely makes your life easier or better. If the honest answer is no, that’s a good sign it can be donated. If you feel a little resistance, try imagining the space and calm you’d gain by letting it go; that usually clarifies things without making you feel deprived.\n\nFor the things you keep, give each one a clear home based on where you naturally reach for it. If something is constantly floating around with no obvious place to land, that’s usually a sign you don’t need it. Once everything has a home, do tiny resets: a two‑ or three‑minute sweep at the end of your day where you put things back. That’s what prevents the slow creep into chaos.\n\nThe goal isn’t to own as little as possible; it’s to own the right amount for the life you actually enjoy living.\n[user] I finally caved and bought one of those tiny apartments with almost no storage—between the downsizing and the building’s strict clutter rules, I spent the weekend photographing everything I’m keeping and mailing the rest to my sister so I’m not tripping over boxes anymore. Given that, can you suggest a simple “one in, one out” (or similar) rule that actually works in a small space, and how I should handle the photos/records so they don’t become their own kind of clutter?\n[assistant] A tiny place almost forces you to build habits that feel automatic, so the simpler the rule, the better. A good version of “one in, one out” for a small space is actually “one in, one gone before it enters.” That means whenever something new is about to cross your threshold—clothes, kitchen tools, books—you decide what it replaces before you even bring it inside. If nothing feels worth letting go of, that’s your signal that you don’t have room for the new thing.\n\nFor the photos and records, treat them like you would treat physical items: store them in one clearly labeled folder, keep only the essential shots, and do a quick sweep every month or so to delete duplicates or anything you no longer need. If you make that folder your single home for all item photos, it won’t swell into digital chaos; it’ll just be a lightweight reference you rarely have to think about.\n[user] Can you make that “one in, one gone before it enters” rule more specific with a few categories (clothes, kitchen gadgets, books/paper, toiletries), and also give me a concrete setup for the photo records (folder structure + naming convention + when to delete/archive) that won’t take more than like 10 minutes a week to maintain?\n[assistant] You can keep the rule tight but still tailored to each type of thing. With clothes, the moment you think about bringing in a new piece, choose the specific item it replaces—a shirt for a shirt, shoes for shoes—and set the outgoing one aside before you buy or bring the new one home. For kitchen gadgets, treat anything with a plug or a single specialty use the same way: if a new tool comes in, the one it makes redundant has to go first. With books and paper, decide in advance whether the new arrival replaces a similar book or whether it needs to be read and passed along within a set window; if you don’t have space for a trade, it stays out. For toiletries, keep a strict “one open, one backup” limit, so a new product only enters if something has just been used up or discarded.\n\nFor the photo records, imagine a single master folder called “Home Inventory,” with just a few subfolders: clothes, kitchen, books, and everything‑else. Name each file with the item type plus a short descriptor and the month and year, something like “jacket_black_puffer_03‑26.” When you let something go, delete the photo immediately so the folder mirrors real life. Once a month, do a quick pass to clear duplicates or anything you no longer care about. That little routine is usually well under ten minutes and keeps the digital side as lean as the physical space.\n[user] Could you tweak the photo system for someone who uses both iPhone Photos and Google Drive (so I don’t end up with duplicates everywhere), and suggest the quickest weekly “inventory review” checklist I can run through in under 10 minutes?\n[assistant] You can keep the photo system smooth by choosing one place as the “official home” and letting the other act only as the capture tool. Since iPhone Photos is where you naturally take pictures, use it just for snapping. Once a week, move the new item photos into a single Google Drive folder called Home Inventory and delete them from your phone right after they upload. That keeps Google Drive as the clean, master record and prevents the two libraries from drifting out of sync.\n\nInside Drive, keep the same simple structure—just a few subfolders that match your major categories. Use the same short naming pattern as before so everything is easy to skim. If you ever need to check something, you always open Drive first, not Photos, so your brain has a single place to look.\n\nFor a quick weekly review, think of it as a tiny reset rather than a full audit. Open Drive, confirm the photos match what you actually still own, remove anything for items you recently let go of, add the week’s new photos if there are any, and clear out any strays still sitting in iPhone Photos. It’s basically: match reality, tidy the folder, empty the camera roll. Done slowly, it’s still under ten minutes, and after a couple weeks it becomes automatic.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still generally avoid streaming subscriptions because they prefer owning physical copies of movies and music?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these five fields:\n{\n  \"decision\": \"ANSWER | UNKNOWN | REFUSE\",\n  \"state_resolution\": \"STALE_DETECTED | STALE_NOT_DETECTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"premise_resistance\": \"FALSE_PREMISE_REJECTED | FALSE_PREMISE_ACCEPTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"action_adaptation\": \"CURRENT_STATE_ALIGNED | STALE_STATE_ALIGNED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"answer\": \"brief answer in the question language, or null\"\n}\n\nField requirements:\n- decision is ANSWER when the selected evidence supports a direct answer.\n- decision is UNKNOWN when evidence is insufficient.\n- decision is REFUSE only for a policy refusal.\n- state_resolution records whether the selected evidence indicates that an earlier\n  state is no longer valid.\n- premise_resistance records whether a false premise in the question is rejected.\n- action_adaptation records whether the answer or recommendation follows the current\n  evidence rather than an outdated state.\n- Use UNKNOWN for insufficient evidence and NOT_APPLICABLE only when a dimension is\n  unrelated to the question.\n- If decision is not ANSWER, answer must be null. If decision is ANSWER, answer must\n  be a non-empty string.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}